computing/INSTALL

These instructions create and install a copy of Theory of Computing.   Note
that I ran these on a spare laptop in 2025-May, so these have been tested.

These are written for Linux because that is what I have.  I expect they
also work on a Mac.  However, if you have Windows then I'm sorry, I cannot
help you.

Step 0) Fresh Ubuntu 24 install, from scratch.  Fresh full TeX Live, from
https://tug.org/texlive/quickinstall.html (although I believe that the apt
package texlive-full will also work).  Also install git, make, Ghostscript,
Python, and Racket.

1) Clone the book's git repo.  In a terminal do this.
  $ cd ~/Documents
  $ git clone https://gitlab.com/jim.hefferon/toc.git computing
(You can call the top-level directory either 'computing' or 'toc' or
'toc-master'. Best is 'computing' is best because it is what I use so it is
the most tested.  Thus your tree of files will look like computing/src/... and
computing/bin/... etc.  Other names will cause Asymptote files to fail.)

2) Set up Asymptote environment.  Put the following lines at the end of your
.profile in your home directory (change "/Documents/computing" to wherever
you put the repo).

# Directories for the Asymptote drawing program
export ASYMPTOTE_DIR=$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/share:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/asy-graphtheory-master/modules:$ASYMPTOTE_DIR

  Log out and log in again (this runs the profile).  You can check that the
  prior works by running "echo $ASYMPTOTE_DIR" in a terminal.

3) Check that Asymptote is working.
    $ cd ~/Documents/computing
    $ cd src/background/asy/3d
    $ asy 3d
  If it compiles into 3d000.pdf without comment  and you can view it in a
  PDF viewer then you are all set.

  Asymptote can complain it is missing libglut3.0.  I did this.
    $ cd /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
    $ ls libglut*
    $ sudo ln -s libglut.3.0.12 libglut.3.0
  
4) Make the book.
  $ cd ~/Documents/computing/src
  $ make 2>&1 | tee Makefile.log
Then wait.  It can take a long time.  On a reasonable machine it can take
ten minutes or more.  I have a very old laptop and it took hours.  At the end
you have book.pdf and answers.pdf.

The command line "2>&1" stuff gives you a .log file to examine
if there is some trouble.  Sometimes I find that I need more packages from
TeX Live.  The other thing that can happen is that sometimes you get a
footnote "counter too large" message and a question mark prompt.  In this case
type x and hit Enter, and then "pdflatex book" should work; what has happened
is that on the first run LaTeX does not know that footnotes are per-page
and it can run out of footnotes in the counter but on the second run it is OK.

I find that sometimes Asymptote does not compile all of a source.  When make
runs LaTeX on book.tex it may say "automata/asy/machine/machine32.pdf not found"
or some such.  But then if you go to the relevant directory
  $ cd automata/asy/machines
  $ asy machines
it will create the machine32.pdf file, and now cd ~/Documents/computing/src
followed by make all works fine.  I don't understand this Heisenbug.

5) Optional: You can get the slides as here.
  $ make slides
  If you get told that background05.pdf is not there, run
"touch slides/background/asy/background.asy" and
"make slides/background/asy/background00.pdf" amd then "make slides" again.
I don't understand this bug.  (A misconception of mine on timing in make?)
 


